      strength: "Medium–Full. The first version of 1996 was a chiefly Honduran blend sold on the promise of \"full body that does not bite\". From the second generation of 2013 onwards the blends have been built around Ecuadorian Sumatra and Nicaraguan leaf; the Carenas of 2021, with a Nicaraguan wrapper over a Honduran Broadleaf binder, leans medium, while the Reserva Especial and the Serie G run richer. Neither the origins of the leaf nor the range of strength agrees with the Cuban version, which is wholly of Cuban leaf and settles at about medium.",
      founded: "The Cuban original's trademark was registered in 1940 by the British importers Nathan Silverstone and Michael De Keyser (having first been recorded by the Zamora y Guerra Co. of Havana), with the brand reaching the market in the late 1940s. The non-Cuban version's US trademark was applied for in 1980 (filed 24 March; first use 6 February; registered 12 January 1982), and the actual cigars were launched as a Honduran product in May 1996",
      founder: "The non-Cuban trademark was held by the importer Max Rohr Imports Inc. The 1996 blend was designed by Christian Eiroa (later of Camacho and C.L.E. Cigars), then in his twenties and working with his father Julio Eiroa at the Tabacos Rancho Jamastran factory in Honduras. The current products are directed by Altadis U.S.A.'s blending collective, the Grupo de Maestros, and by Rafael Nodal, head of product development",
      meaning: "Meaning 'Saint Louis the King'. The spelling, splicing the English 'Saint' with the Spanish 'Luis', is distinctive. Two theories of the name's origin coexist — the town of San Luis in Pinar del Río province, and Thornton Wilder's 1927 novel 'The Bridge of San Luis Rey' — with neither decisive",
