      trivia: "【Not a Brand Divided by the Embargo】Whereas the non-Cuban versions of Punch and Hoyo de Monterrey arose directly from the 1962 embargo on Cuba, the non-Cuban Saint Luis Rey's US trademark was filed on 24 March 1980 (first use 6 February; registered 12 January 1982) and the actual launch came only in May 1996. It is one of the few cases where the stock formula of 'twins split by the embargo' does not apply — a late-onset division in which a separate rights-holder secured the name in the United States while the Cuban original remained in rude health.\n【Twenty Years When One Company Held Both Kings】Altadis acquired 50 per cent of Habanos S.A. in 2000 and thereafter, until 2020, was involved both on the side selling the Cuban Saint Luis Rey to the world and on the side holding the non-Cuban version's US trademark. For a long period, then, the structure differed from the 'two hostile owners' of Punch and Hoyo. With the division of 2020, however, the US and non-US sides became separate legal entities, and that explanation no longer holds.\n【The First Blender Was Christian Eiroa, in His Twenties】The designer of the 1996 original was Christian Eiroa, then in his twenties and working with his father Julio at the Rancho Jamastran factory in Honduras — the man who would later raise Camacho to world rank and found C.L.E. Cigars. With the 30th-anniversary edition of 2026, he returned to the marque after thirty years.\n【A 'Honduran Version' with a Dominican-made Line】The Tabacales, released in 2025, is made at Tabacalera de García in La Romana, Dominican Republic — the first line of the marque ever made outside Honduras. Note that the label 'Honduran Saint Luis Rey' no longer implies a single country of manufacture.\n【A Marque That Passed through Sanctions, Factory and All】In October 2025, in the wake of the US sanctions against Chen Zhi, Flor de Copán — under Allied Cigar — was cut off from dealing with US companies, and production and shipment stopped. Only after the issue of provisional licences by OFAC (valid to 2028) and OFSI (valid to 2031) did shipments resume, in May 2026."
